## Visual design requirements

Typography

- A deliberate type hierarchy.
- Excellent readability on small screens.
- Consistent line heights and spacing.
- A restrained selection of typefaces.
- Proper font weights rather than simulated boldness.

Color

- A coherent brand palette.
- Accessible text/background contrast.
- Deliberate light and dark themes, if both are offered.
- Consistent colors for buttons, links, borders, backgrounds and status indicators.

Layout

- Consistent spacing and alignment.
- Well-defined content widths.
- Balanced whitespace.
- Responsive grids.
- Consistent navigation, footers and page templates.
- No accidental horizontal scrolling.

Motion

- Subtle transitions.
- Functional hover and focus states.
- No animation that delays access to important information.
- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.
- Avoid unnecessary scroll-triggered animations.

Visual assets

- Actual company work.
- Architecture illustrations.
- Custom diagrams.
- Real product interface screenshots.
- Genuine project evidence.
- Custom-designed icons and visual elements.

Avoid generic stock photographs, meaningless gradients, excessive glassmorphism and decorative animations that do not communicate anything.

## Design system

Establish a real design system before building every page.

| Design-system layer | What it defines                             |
| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |
| Foundations         | Colors, typography, spacing, grids          |
| Components          | Buttons, links, cards, forms                |
| Navigation          | Header, mobile navigation, footer           |
| Feedback            | Loading, success, error and disabled states |
| Interaction         | Hover, focus, active and transition states  |
| Responsive          | Breakpoints and layout behavior             |
| Accessibility       | Contrast, focus, keyboard and motion        |

Use design tokens rather than arbitrary CSS values throughout the project.

I would start with Astro components and custom CSS/design tokens. Tailwind is optional. It is not a requirement for production quality.

Similarly, shadcn/ui is not a requirement. It is a React-oriented component approach, and adding React solely to use it would be unnecessary for your mostly static website.

## The visual quality standard

Every major page should undergo:

- Desktop visual review.
- Mobile visual review.
- Tablet visual review.
- Real-device testing.
- Light/dark theme testing, if supported.
- Keyboard interaction testing.
- Screenshot regression testing.

Do not approve a design just because it looks good in a desktop screenshot.

# 5. Information architecture and company content

Your website should communicate four things immediately:

1. What EULOK builds.
2. Which customers EULOK serves.
3. What business problems EULOK solves.
4. Why a customer should trust EULOK with a software project.

I recommend this structure.

## Recommended website structure

01\. Home

Positioning, core services, selected work, engineering credibility and contact CTA.

02\. Services

Dedicated pages for software engineering, web applications, integrations, automation, API development and software rescue where these are genuine offerings.

03\. Work / Case studies

Actual projects, challenges, solutions, engineering decisions and measurable results.

04\. About / Company

Company background, engineering philosophy, location, operating principles and accountability.

05\. How we work

Discovery, scoping, proposal, development, verification, delivery and support.

06\. Insights

Technical articles, engineering decisions, research, implementation lessons and build logs.

07\. Contact

Project inquiry, contact details, email and WhatsApp.

08\. Legal

Privacy policy, terms, cookie information where relevant and applicable disclosures.

## What the homepage should accomplish

The homepage should not attempt to explain the entire company.

It should have:

- A strong, concrete hero statement.
- A short explanation of the services.
- A clear indication of who you work with.
- A few selected projects or evidence of engineering capability.
- A short explanation of your engineering approach.
- A clear contact action.
- A consistent footer.

For example, rather than:

> Empowering the future through digital transformation.

Use something grounded in the actual work:

> We engineer software that solves complex business problems.

The final copy should be more differentiated than this example, but the principle is important.

## Case studies are especially important

International clients cannot easily evaluate a new Ugandan software company through an office visit or personal introduction.

Your website therefore needs evidence.

A proper case study should include:

| Section         | Evidence                                                |
| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
| Client/context  | Who the solution served, subject to permission          |
| Problem         | The actual business or technical problem                |
| Constraints     | Budget, scale, legacy systems and technical limitations |
| Solution        | What was engineered                                     |
| Architecture    | Relevant technologies and design decisions              |
| Verification    | Tests, performance, reliability and security evidence   |
| Outcome         | Measurable business or technical improvement            |
| Further details | Relevant screenshots, diagrams or documentation         |

Never invent clients, testimonials, performance results or project outcomes.

A well-documented internal or open-source project can also demonstrate engineering ability, provided it is clearly identified as such.

## International credibility versus Ugandan identity

I would not conceal Uganda.

Instead:

- Show Kampala, Uganda as your operating base.
- Clearly state that you serve clients internationally.
- Use a professional company email address.
- Provide a consistent contact identity.
- State your working hours in East Africa Time where relevant.
- Show actual registration and business credentials when available.
- Explain how projects are managed remotely.
- Publish genuine engineering work.

Uganda should be an identifying advantage, not something that makes the business look geographically limited.

