No weakened tests to make a deployment pass. No ignored failures without documented justification.

# 10. Analytics, monitoring and reliability

You need to know what visitors experience, not merely whether Cloudflare reports that a deployment succeeded.

## Recommended monitoring

Performance

- Real-user Core Web Vitals.
- Page loading performance.
- JavaScript errors.
- Image and asset failures.
- Mobile versus desktop experience.

Availability

- HTTP uptime monitoring.
- SSL certificate monitoring.
- DNS resolution checks.
- Critical route checks.
- Contact-form health checks.

Business analytics

- Number of inquiries.
- Contact form completion rate.
- Service-page engagement.
- Traffic by country.
- Referral sources.
- Conversion paths.

Privacy

- Collect only necessary analytics data.
- Avoid unnecessary tracking scripts.
- Document the purpose and retention of collected information.
- Configure consent where legally required.

Cloudflare Web Analytics is worth evaluating for the public website. You can use a separate error-monitoring service later if the complexity justifies it.

## About the 99.99% availability target

99.99% availability permits only approximately 52.6 minutes of downtime per year.

That is not a sensible promise to put on your website simply because you use Cloudflare.

Instead:

- Establish an availability objective.
- Measure actual downtime.
- Define how failures are detected.
- Have a deployment rollback procedure.
- Monitor third-party dependencies.
- Keep the website functional when nonessential services fail.

A static website is easier to make resilient because its public pages do not depend on a continuously running application server.

